From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 08:58:29 +0900
Subject: Unify the capture variables of an overriding method's parameters

Under capture checking, a class in the expansion of an inline method could
not override a method whose parameter type came from a type argument that was
inferred for the call:

    inline def derived[value](x: value): Inspectable { type Self = value } =
      new Inspectable:
        type Self = value
        def text(arg: value): String = "x"

    def test(point: Point) = derived(point)

    error overriding method text in trait Inspectable of type (value: Self): String;
      method text of type (arg: Point^'s1): String has incompatible type

An inferred type argument of an inline call is marked `@inferred`, and Setup
gives each occurrence of it in the expansion a capture set variable of its
own: one in the parameter type of `text`, another in the alias of `Self`.
Overrides are checked with frozen constraints, under which an unsolved
variable is related to nothing but itself, and parameter types must be the
same. The variables in a member's signature are otherwise those of its
inferred result type, solved when the member is checked; nothing solved or
related these.

The same happens without a class in the source where a lambda is converted
to a SAM type for which the platform makes a class: under `-scalajs` any
trait, since `ExpandSAMs` runs before capture checking. There a typeclass
instance written as a lambda in an inline method was rejected, though the
JVM, which keeps the closure, accepted it.

Before the overrides of a class are checked, the parameter types of each
overriding pair are now compared without freezing, which unifies their
variables, and the variables are then solved. All pairs are unified before
any variable is solved, so that the order of the pairs does not matter, and
solving is what makes the frozen check succeed, since `asSeenFrom` maps an
unsolved variable to a new one each time a member's info is taken.

The unification is an attempt, and must leave nothing behind if it fails.
A variable that could not take an element withdrew it from itself but not
from the sets depending on it which had already accepted it, where the
comparer, trying the comparison another way, found it and succeeded. An
inclusion is therefore atomic while unifying (`CCState.withAtomicIncludes`);
elsewhere a failed comparison is an error and nothing changes.

Written with LLM assistance (Claude Code).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: overridevars
